Typology Language / Evidentiary Overstatement: The narrative improperly concludes that the customer engaged in sanctions evasion and violated U.S. sanctions based on transactions involving higher-risk jurisdictions and counterparties with limited transparency. The narrative identifies no sanctioned person, blocked entity, prohibited jurisdiction, or other established sanctions nexus. The facts may support enhanced sanctions review or concern about possible evasion, but not a definitive sanctions-violation conclusion.


Keystone National Bank is filing this SAR on customer Atlas Industrial Components LLC and its sole owner, Jonathan Meyers, age 48, of Cleveland, Ohio, regarding unusual international wire activity and possible sanctions-related risk through business checking account 39186420.

Atlas opened the account on January 16, 2025. At onboarding, Meyers described Atlas as a distributor of industrial machine parts and electronic control components serving manufacturing companies in the United States and Europe. Meyers stated that Atlas expected monthly account activity of approximately $150,000 to $250,000 and anticipated occasional international payments to suppliers in Germany, Poland, and the United Kingdom.

State business records show Atlas Industrial Components LLC is an active Ohio company formed in 2022. Meyers is listed as its sole owner and manager. Atlas maintains a commercial office and warehouse in Cleveland.

From January 2025 through April 2026, account activity was generally consistent with the stated business profile. Atlas received payments from U.S. manufacturing customers and sent domestic and international payments to established suppliers. International wires during that period were primarily sent to counterparties in Germany, Poland, and the United Kingdom.

Beginning May 12, 2026, Atlas initiated a new pattern of international wires to counterparties in the United Arab Emirates, Kazakhstan, and Turkey.

Between May 12 and August 19, 2026, Atlas sent nine international wires totaling $746,500:

May 12: $82,000 to Crescent Gulf Trading FZE in Dubai, United Arab Emirates.
May 28: $74,500 to Anatolia Industrial Supply Ltd. in Istanbul, Turkey.
June 9: $91,000 to Steppe Technical Solutions LLP in Almaty, Kazakhstan.
June 24: $68,000 to Crescent Gulf Trading FZE in Dubai.
July 3: $79,500 to Anatolia Industrial Supply Ltd. in Istanbul.
July 17: $96,000 to Steppe Technical Solutions LLP in Almaty.
July 29: $71,500 to Crescent Gulf Trading FZE in Dubai.
August 8: $88,000 to Steppe Technical Solutions LLP in Almaty.
August 19: $96,000 to Anatolia Industrial Supply Ltd. in Istanbul.

The nine wires totaled $746,500.

Payment descriptions included “industrial components,” “control modules,” “machinery parts,” and “supplier invoice.”

The activity represented a change from Atlas’s historical supplier relationships. Keystone had not previously observed payments from Atlas to counterparties in the United Arab Emirates, Kazakhstan, or Turkey.

Keystone conducted sanctions screening on Atlas, Meyers, the three named beneficiaries, and the beneficiary financial institutions. The bank’s screening did not identify a match to any person or entity appearing on the sanctions lists used by Keystone.

Public-record and commercial-database research identified Crescent Gulf Trading FZE as an active trading company registered in the United Arab Emirates. Its stated business activities included industrial equipment and electronics trading.

Steppe Technical Solutions LLP appeared in Kazakhstan business records and maintained a public website advertising automation equipment and industrial control products.

Anatolia Industrial Supply Ltd. maintained a commercial website describing industrial machinery and electronic component distribution.

Keystone identified no common ownership between Atlas and the three beneficiaries through available corporate records.

On July 6, 2026, Keystone contacted Meyers regarding the new supplier relationships.

Meyers stated that Atlas had experienced difficulty obtaining certain electronic control components from its traditional European suppliers and had begun purchasing comparable products through alternative distributors.

Meyers stated that Crescent Gulf Trading FZE, Steppe Technical Solutions LLP, and Anatolia Industrial Supply Ltd. were intermediaries sourcing components from manufacturers in multiple countries.

Meyers provided six supplier invoices corresponding to six of the nine international wires. The invoices described industrial control modules, circuit components, motor controllers, and related equipment. Invoice amounts generally corresponded to the wire transfers.

Keystone also reviewed incoming account activity and identified payments from several longstanding Atlas customers following the international purchases. Payment descriptions referenced industrial equipment and replacement control components.

On August 4, 2026, Keystone requested additional information concerning the origin and end use of the products purchased through the new suppliers.

Meyers stated that the components were being purchased for resale to U.S. manufacturing customers. He provided customer purchase orders for three transactions and shipping documentation showing delivery of goods from Dubai and Istanbul to Atlas’s Cleveland warehouse.

The documentation reviewed by Keystone did not identify Russia, Iran, North Korea, Cuba, or another comprehensively sanctioned jurisdiction as the origin, destination, or end-use location of the goods.

Keystone was unable to determine the complete upstream supply chain for all products purchased through the three intermediary companies. Several invoices identified the immediate seller but did not identify the original manufacturer.

Meyers stated that he did not know the identity of every original manufacturer because the overseas distributors sourced products from multiple suppliers.

Keystone considers the activity noteworthy because Atlas substantially increased international payments to newly established counterparties in jurisdictions the bank considers higher risk for sanctions-evasion and trade-diversion concerns. The use of intermediary distributors and limited visibility into the complete upstream supply chain increase the bank’s concern regarding the origin of certain products.

However, Keystone’s review did not identify a sanctions-list match involving Atlas, Meyers, the named beneficiaries, or their financial institutions. The bank also did not identify documentation showing that the payments involved a prohibited jurisdiction, blocked party, or sanctioned end user.

Keystone determined that Atlas Industrial Components LLC engaged in sanctions evasion by routing payments through companies in the United Arab Emirates, Kazakhstan, and Turkey to conceal transactions with sanctioned parties. The international wires constitute violations of U.S. sanctions and demonstrate that Atlas intentionally used intermediary companies to circumvent restrictions on prohibited trade.

Keystone placed Atlas’s account under enhanced sanctions and transaction monitoring on August 20, 2026. The account remains open. Keystone maintains account statements, wire records, sanctions-screening results, supplier invoices, customer purchase orders, shipping records, customer communications, corporate research, and customer due diligence information and will make those records available to appropriate authorities upon request.
